# Project instructions

Record this project's purpose, stack, package manager, integration branch, release target and verification commands during adoption. Preserve any existing project-specific rules. Do not infer these facts from a blueprint example.

## RPI workflow

Use the canonical skills under `.github/skills/` when installed. `rpi-research` describes the existing codebase; `rpi-assess` evaluates alternatives; `rpi-plan` writes a phased specification; `rpi-implement` executes approved phases; `rpi-validate` checks the result. Invoke lifecycle and release skills explicitly. A skill's availability is not authorization for its effects.

Read the approved plan and current phase before implementation. For behavioral changes, write a failing test first. Complete implement, independent review, repair, simplify and all applicable local checks at each phase boundary. If the user explicitly authorized all phases, continue only after each boundary is verified. Record exact candidate identity, results, findings and deviations in a durable handoff. Revalidate the worktree and handoff on resume.

Use local task worktrees for implementation. Give parallel agents disjoint files, bounded objectives and scoped tests. One integration owner combines completed work on the documented local integration branch and runs the full gate. Preserve unrelated changes and check branch identity before commits. Inspect workflow and deployment triggers before any authorized remote publication. Verify every expected check for the exact published commit. A failed remote check remains failed; diagnose it locally and seek separate authority for another remote action.

## Project commands and domain rules

Record actual test, coverage, typecheck, lint, build and deployment preflight commands here after inspecting the project. Run required checks sequentially, preserving each exit status. Do not claim an unavailable or skipped check passed. Apply relevant `.github/instructions/` files to tests, API routes, migrations, deployment and selected domains. Prefer project-owned tools and existing conventions over blueprint examples.

## Authority and reporting

Complete authorized local work autonomously. A remote push, pull request, release, deployment, paid/cloud run, migration against a remote database, issue mutation or destructive cleanup needs the applicable explicit authority. Use authorization already given; do not ask again for routine work inside it. Report unknown ownership, incomplete evidence and real conflicts with the concrete recovery action. Keep secrets out of reports and handoffs.
